{
  "id": "fa_ungegn_2012",
  "title": "Persian — UN 2012 (Iranian national system)",
  "language": "fa",
  "script": "Arab",
  "kind": "official",
  "status": "current",
  "year": 2012,
  "authority": "United Nations resolution X/8 (2012); Iranian 'broad transcription' system (developed since 1988, officially approved in Iran in 2003)",
  "description": "Pronunciation-based system used in Iran's national gazetteer and recommended by the UN for Persian geographical names: short vowels a e o, long ā i u, diphthongs ey ow, č š ž x, q for both غ and ق, homophone letters merged (ث س ص = s; ذ ز ض ظ = z; ت ط = t; ح ه = h), ’ for ʿayn and hamza (not romanized word-initially), final silent h dropped (Sāve).",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.eki.ee/wgrs/rom1_fa.pdf",
    "https://unstats.un.org/unsd/geoinfo/ungegn/docs/10th-uncsgn-docs/econf/E_CONF.101_118_Rev.1_Persian%20romanization.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Persian"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Not reversible by design: letters pronounced alike are romanized alike.",
    "Ezafe: -e after a consonant, -ye after a vowel or silent h (Gol-e Sorx, Dahane-ye Mambar); it is produced only when written (kasra at the end of a word, ۀ, هٔ).",
    "Arabic article: ol, assimilated before sun letters, written as a separate lower-case word (Zeyn od Din); names ending in Allāh are written as one word with o (Abdollāh) by analogy with BGN/PCGN — the UN summary does not cover this case.",
    "ZWNJ (U+200C) inside a name is removed and the parts are joined (Kāzemzāde); the UN summary does not say how compounds written with ZWNJ are divided.",
    "خوا is xā (silent و dropped) — inferred from the system's pronunciation basis, not stated in the UNGEGN summary.",
    "Arabic-script forms from the name lexicon (with ة, ي, ى) are accepted: ة after a short vowel = -e, ي/ى are folded onto ی."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤئابپتثجچحخدذرزژسشصضطظعغفقکگلمنوهیةۀ",
  "hook": "arabic",
